                <pb facs="0530" n="428" sameAs="frus:frus1908/pg428"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 2126/51.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >British Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1908-01-20"><hi rendition="#italic">January 20,
                        1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 16.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to-day received telegraphic
                    information of His Majesty’s Government to the effect that His Majesty’s consul
                    general at Port au Prince reports the outbreak of a serious revolution on the
                    island of Haiti. According to this information the ports of Gonaives and St.
                    Marc are in a state of blockade and the lives and property of British subjects
                    resident in those towns are endangered. It is further reported that a United
                    States naval vessel is on the spot and that His Majesty’s ship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Indefatigable</hi> has been ordered to proceed there
                    without delay, but can not well arrive in less than a week.</p>
                <p>I am instructed further to inquire whether the United States Government would be
                    so good as to instruct their naval authorities to afford protection to British
                    subjects in the island of Haiti pending the arrival of H. M. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Indefatigable</hi> to such extent as may be
                    requisite.</p>
                <p>I shall be greatly obliged if I might be put in a position to assure my
                    Government on this point as soon as conveniently possible.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James Bryce.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
